                          Git Revert Conflict — Practical Notes

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


git log --oneline -4  -->  View recent commits
git show d69f472   -->  Inspect a commit
git show d69f472:Git/gitcommands  --> View the file content from that commit

*Resolve the conflict
If the intended result is to delete Git/gitcommands as part of the revert:
git rm Git/gitcommands
git revert --continue

*Restore the file after reverting
If I want the file back in the current working tree:
git restore --source=d69f472 -- Git/gitcommands


Command	Purpose
git revert <commit>	Creates a new commit that reverses the selected commit's changes
git revert --abort	Cancels a revert operation that is still in progress
git restore <file>	Restores file content in the working tree
git restore --source=<commit> -- <file>	Restores a file from a specified commit



